\paragraph{Results.}
RV and ET reach $\leq$0.03\% for all models on both datasets.
DR drops 76--99\% on SciERC and 42--90\% on DocRED; R-F1 improves in 5/6 settings (DocRED: Qwen $+$0.50\,pp, $p{<}0.001$; Llama $+$0.29\,pp, $p{=}0.013$).
CF direction depends on calibration: S1 introduces CF for calibrated models (up to 12.02\%; \S\ref{sec:discussion}) but reduces it for Mistral (56.87\%\,$\to$\,1.27\%).
Fine-tuning resolves both: DR\,$\leq$\,0.09\%, CF\,$\leq$\,1.18\%, R-F1 $\pm$0.6\,pp (\S\ref{sec:composability}).

\paragraph{Mistral as boundary condition.}
When entity typing quality degrades substantially (Mistral: ET 1.5--7.6\%, DR 56.9--57.4\% unconstrained), DR reduction is limited to 42\% vs.\ 76--99\% for calibrated models.

\paragraph{Post-hoc filtering comparison.}
Post-hoc filtering achieves DR${=}$0\% by discarding violating triples but with 15--74\% rejection rates; on DocRED (Qwen), post-hoc discards 48\% of predictions while OntoCoDec retains all with DR 10.21\% (Appendix~\ref{app:posthoc-extended}).
Under fine-tuning, both methods achieve comparable F1.

% Section: Constraint Composition (compressed, 787→~500 words)

At each decoding step $t$, let $G(t) \subseteq V$ and $S(t) \subseteq V$ denote the tokens permitted by the grammar and semantic layers, respectively.
The final mask is their conjunction:
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:composition}
  M_{\text{final}}(t) = M_{\text{grammar}}(t) \;\wedge\; M_{\text{semantic}}(t),
\end{equation}
%
where $\wedge$ denotes element-wise AND over the vocabulary $V$; a token is allowed only if it satisfies \emph{both} structural and type-level constraints.
The conjunction is asymmetric: $S(t)$ is defined only over $G(t)$. This reflects the enabling dependency (\S\ref{sec:method}): the semantic layer requires the relation type to belong to $\mathcal{R}$, a property guaranteed only by the grammar layer.

\paragraph{Grammar-First Fallback.}
When $S(t) \cap G(t) = \emptyset$ (tight constraints leave no token that is both grammar-valid and semantically valid), na\"{\i}ve masking would force selection of a grammar-invalid token, breaking JSON structure.
We enforce a \emph{grammar-first fallback}: semantic masking is skipped and the grammar layer alone controls selection.
Structural integrity is a hard constraint; type violations are soft errors detectable by downstream validation, with DR (ontologically invalid type pair) more severe than CF (individually valid triples with cross-triple type inconsistency).

\paragraph{Constraint Tightness.}
Recall the tightness of relation $r$ (\S\ref{sec:bg:ontology}):\footnote{For symmetric relations, $|\sigma(r)|$ counts ordered pairs; the normalization $|\mathcal{E}|^2$ remains appropriate since we evaluate on directed triples.}
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:tightness}
  \kappa(r) = 1 - \frac{|\sigma(r)|}{|\mathcal{E}|^2},
\end{equation}
%
The mean $\bar{\kappa} = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{R}|}\sum_{r} \kappa(r)$ characterizes schema restrictiveness; tighter constraints force more frequent fallback and higher residual DR.
Cross-dataset results align: SciERC ($\bar{\kappa}{=}0.333$) achieves 94--99\% DR reduction vs.\ DocRED ($\bar{\kappa}{=}0.842$) at 45--90\% (full range 42--99\% across three models; Appendix~\ref{app:dual-factor}).
$\kappa$ is necessary but not sufficient---entity type confusability is the critical co-factor (Appendix~\ref{app:kappa-case-study})---and $\bar{\kappa}$ provides deployment guidance ($\bar{\kappa}{<}0.5$: near-complete DR elimination; $\bar{\kappa}{>}0.8$: requires fine-tuning; Appendix~\ref{app:tcr}).

\paragraph{Greedy limitation and fallback cascade.}
\label{sec:greedy-limitation}
The semantic layer commits entity types greedily---an instance of Level~3 (non-local) constraints (\S\ref{sec:decidability})---so CF violations arise when the same entity receives different types across triples; beam search (\S\ref{sec:beam-results}) resolves this.
Fallback frequency correlates with $\bar{\kappa}$: SciERC ($\bar{\kappa}{=}0.333$) triggers fallback rarely; DocRED ($\bar{\kappa}{=}0.842$) more often; fine-tuning narrows the regime to DR\,$\leq$\,0.09\%.
The cascade effect is bounded: fallback triggers on 36.6\% of type-bearing positions (zero-shot SciERC), but \textsc{Generic} assignments weaken DR checks for only 6.3--9.4\% of subsequent relations.
Fallback-affected triples exhibit P${=}$0\% across both datasets; removing them eliminates ${\sim}$10\% of false positives with no recall loss.

\paragraph{Non-rectangularity and independent enforcement.}
\label{sec:rectangularity}
Type signatures are non-rectangular (0/7 SciERC, 23/96 DocRED relations satisfy $\sigma(r) = \operatorname{dom}(r) \times \operatorname{range}(r)$); independent domain/range enforcement is a sound over-approximation of pair-level enforcement: it never rejects a valid pair but admits 33--37\% spurious pairs on non-rectangular relations.
Pair-level enforcement would require $O(|\sigma(r)|)$ prefix matching per relation; we choose independent enforcement for efficiency, noting that fine-tuning eliminates all residual DR on both datasets (Table~\ref{tab:composability}).

